    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d850">

                <pb facs="0922" n="1756" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1756"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.711/2477</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul at Christiania</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Ifft</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 100</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-15">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 15, 1918</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department acknowledges the receipt of
                    your despatch No. 38 of February 12, 1918, enclosing three envelopes addressed
                    to <pb facs="0923" n="1757" xml:id="pg1757"/> you as follows: “The American
                    Consul, Christiania, Norway.”<anchor n="1" xml:id="d850-f1"/> You call the
                    Department’s attention to the fact that these envelopes have been opened by the
                    British censorship authorities and suggest that a protest be made in the
                    premises.</p>
                <p>In reply you are informed that, inasmuch as neither international law nor
                    established practice exempts consular correspondence from censorship no
                    exception can be taken to the action of which you complain.</p>
                <p>Under this Government’s regulations all correspondence to or from foreign consuls
                    in the United States other than that addressed to or emanating from their
                    governments, is subject to censorship. The extention of reciprocal courtesies to
                    American consuls abroad is expected of foreign governments. Failure on the part
                    of a foreign government to extend these courtesies to you should be
                    reported.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilbur J. Carr</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
